(*
# Collision
The `map/zips/collision.zip` renderer: one 256x256 truecolour chunk per region
per plane, named `<plane>-<regionX>-<regionY>.png`. Ported from
`net.runelite.cache.SimbaCollisionMapDumper`.

```pascal
var
  world: TCollisionWorld;
  objs: TCollisionObjects;
  cm: TCollisionMap;
world := TCollisionWorld.FromStore(store);
objs  := TCollisionObjects.Create(store);
cm    := TCollisionMap.Create(TRegion.Load(store, RegionIdOf(50, 50), []), world);
dump.SaveImage('map/collision/' + cm.ChunkName(0), cm.Render(0, objs));
```

## Four colours, and never a fifth
`SimbaCollisionMapDumper.java:58-61` declares exactly four, and a histogram of
the committed zip finds exactly those four and nothing else:

| | ARGB | meaning |
|---|---|---|
| `collisionColor` | `$FF333333` | an object's footprint |
| `wallColor` | `$FF000000` | a blocked tile, or a wall |
| `doorColor` | `$FFFF0000` | a wall that is a door |
| `walkableColor` | `$FFFFFFFF` | |

Simba's `TColor` is **BGR**, so `doorColor` is the one that does not survive a
channel swap. Every write here goes through the individual `.R`/`.G`/`.B`
fields of `TColorBGRA` for that reason, never through a packed literal.

That palette is also what makes the `!= wallColor` test the object fill does
(`:514`, `:528`) collapse to **`R <> 0`**: black is the only one of the four
with a zero red channel.

## The terrain pass is bit 0 of the settings byte, and nothing else
`SimbaCollisionMapDumper.java:298-326` looks like it branches three ways, but
the `(tileSetting and 24) <> 0` arm at `:309` and the fall-through at `:323`
pass the *same* expression to `drawTile`. Only the bridge arm (`:314-321`)
changes anything:

```
walkable := (setting(z, x, y) and 1) = 0
except on plane 0, when (setting(1, x, y) and 2) <> 0 and (setting(z,x,y) and 24) = 0:
  walkable := (setting(1, x, y) and 1) = 0
```

`blockingMask`, `impenetrable`, `solid` and `interactType` play no part at the
tile level.

## Why one region at a time is enough
The Java allocates a single canvas covering every region - 13056 x 45568 px,
2.4 GB - and cuts each chunk out of it (`:280`). Objects genuinely write
outside their own region: `drawY = (drawBaseY + (64 - sizeY - localY)) * 4`
goes negative for a tall object near the north edge, and the type-10..22 fill
overruns the east edge. So the naive reading is that a chunk depends on its
neighbours and cannot be rendered alone.

**It does not, and the reason is the order inside the per-region loop**
(`:276-294`):

```java
drawRegions(image, drawBaseX, drawBaseY, z, region);   // terrain: all 4096 tiles
drawObjects(image, drawBaseX, drawBaseY, region, z);   // objects
... image.getSubimage(...) -> zip                       // exported immediately
```

The terrain pass writes **every** pixel of the region's own 256x256 rectangle
before its objects run. Any bleed an earlier region left inside that rectangle
is therefore erased, and a later region's bleed has not happened yet when the
chunk is exported. A chunk is exactly `terrain(R) + objects(R)`, and nothing
else can ever be visible in it.

This is measured, not assumed: rendering every region alone reproduces
**11732 / 11732** committed chunks byte for byte. Compositing the west
neighbour's objects on top instead - what a "render the 3x3 neighbourhood"
port does - scores **11495 / 11732**, and `0-48-50` / `0-48-48` come out at
65488 and 65472 of 65536 pixels.

Note what that number says about the export order. `RegionLoader.regions` is a
`HashMap` (`region/RegionLoader.java:52`) and region ids are `< 65536`, so
iteration is by bucket `((regionX mod 16) shl 8) or regionY`: the west
neighbour sorts *after* the region for the 155 regions with
`regionX mod 16 = 0`, i.e. for 620 chunks. Those 620 are the chunks whose
missing bleed is easiest to *see*, but they are not the whole story - only 6 of
the 237 chunks the composite gets wrong are among them. A neighbour drawn
*before* the region bleeds just as much; it is the region's own terrain pass
that erases it. So reproducing the export order is not required either.

## What a per-region port must still carry: the global canvas bounds
The bounds checks in the object pass are against the **whole-map** canvas, not
the chunk:

* `drawX + xSize >= width` (`:509`) - `continue`s the whole location, block B
  included, for any object crossing the canvas' east edge.
* `drawY + ySize >= height` (`:510`) - the same at its south edge.
* `drawY - xSize < 0` / `drawX + ySize >= width` (`:524-525`) - block B's
  equivalents.
* `drawX >= 0 and drawY >= 0 and drawX < width and drawY < height` (`:437`) -
  the wall types' single guard.

Measured over cache 2625, only the first of those ever fires (22 times, all in
the easternmost region column), and removing all five leaves every one of the
11732 chunks byte-identical. They are reproduced anyway because they are what
the Java does and a later revision can move an object.

What is *not* optional is that they stay **global**. Rewriting them against
chunk bounds - the obvious thing to do once the canvas is 256x256 - drops the
whole footprint of every object that crosses a chunk edge: measured, that is
`0-48-50` at 65317 / 65536 and hundreds of chunks wrong. {ref}`TCollisionWorld`
carries the canvas dimensions for exactly this, and the writes are clipped
separately.

## Three pieces of the Java that must NOT be ported
1. **The `Close`-op branch** (`:451-459`). `EntityOpsDefinition.Op` is
   `@AllArgsConstructor` with no `@Data`/`@EqualsAndHashCode` (`:84-88`), so it
   inherits identity `equals` and `getOps().contains(new Op("Close"))` is
   *always* false. The guard at `:441-442` is therefore always true and the
   `else` block - which carries its own off-by-one, `drawY + MAP_SCALE` at
   `:456`, one row past the tile - never runs.
2. **`pushDownLocs`** (`:360`) is declared, iterated at `:385` and never filled.
3. **`object.getName() == null`** (`:500`) is unreachable: `ObjectDefinition`
   defaults `name` to the *string* `"null"` (`definitions/ObjectDefinition.java:39`),
   which is also what `TObjectDefinition.Default` does.

## The BLACK_CHUNK substitution
`:283-290`. A chunk that came out fully walkable and is **outside** the
mainland box (`z = 0`, `regionX` 15..62, `regionY` 32..65) is replaced wholesale
by an all-black image. Over the committed zip: 481 all-white chunks exist and
every one is inside the box; the one all-black chunk inside the box is
genuinely all-blocked.

{ref}`TCollisionMap.Render` applies it, because it is part of what gets written
to the zip. {ref}`TCollisionMap.RenderRaw` is the collision layer without it.
*)
{$DEFINE WL_CACHE_DUMP_COLLISION_INCLUDED}
{$IFNDEF WL_CACHE_REGION_INCLUDED}{$INCLUDE_ONCE ../../region.simba}{$ENDIF}
{$IFNDEF WL_CACHE_DEF_OBJECT_INCLUDED}{$INCLUDE_ONCE ../../defs/object.simba}{$ENDIF}

const
  // net.runelite.cache.SimbaCollisionMapDumper.MAP_SCALE
  COLLISION_SCALE: Integer = 4;
  // Region.X * MAP_SCALE - the side of one exported chunk, in pixels.
  COLLISION_CHUNK_SIZE: Integer = 256;

  // Wintertodt's invisible, non-colliding walls, skipped by id at :431-433.
  COLLISION_WINTERTODT_ID: Integer = 24720;

  // mainlandX1/Y1/X2/Y2 (:75-78). Region coordinates, inclusive.
  COLLISION_MAINLAND_X1: Integer = 15;
  COLLISION_MAINLAND_Y1: Integer = 32;
  COLLISION_MAINLAND_X2: Integer = 62;
  COLLISION_MAINLAND_Y2: Integer = 65;

type
(*
## TCollisionWorld
```pascal
TCollisionWorld = record
  MinRegionX, MinRegionY, MaxRegionX, MaxRegionY: Integer;
  Width, Height: Integer;   // the whole-map canvas, in pixels
end;
```
The extent of the region set being dumped, i.e. what `RegionLoader.getLowestX()`
and friends answer (`region/RegionLoader.java:177-200`) and what
`SimbaCollisionMapDumper.drawRegions` turns into a canvas size (`:202-212`).

It exists only so the object pass' bounds checks can stay global while the
writes are clipped to one chunk - see the unit docstring. On cache 2625 it is
x 15..65, y 19..196, i.e. 13056 x 45568 px.
*)
  TCollisionWorld = record
    MinRegionX, MinRegionY, MaxRegionX, MaxRegionY: Integer;
    Width, Height: Integer;
  end;

(*
## TCollisionWorld.Create
```pascal
function TCollisionWorld.Create(minX, minY, maxX, maxY: Integer): TCollisionWorld; static;
```
The canvas spanned by region coordinates `minX..maxX` by `minY..maxY`,
inclusive.
*)
function TCollisionWorld.Create(minX, minY, maxX, maxY: Integer): TCollisionWorld; static;
begin
  if (maxX < minX) or (maxY < minY) then
    raise 'Collision: empty region bounds ' + ToStr(minX) + '..' + ToStr(maxX) +
          ', ' + ToStr(minY) + '..' + ToStr(maxY);

  Result.MinRegionX := minX;
  Result.MinRegionY := minY;
  Result.MaxRegionX := maxX;
  Result.MaxRegionY := maxY;
  Result.Width := (maxX - minX + 1) * REGION_X * COLLISION_SCALE;
  Result.Height := (maxY - minY + 1) * REGION_Y * COLLISION_SCALE;
end;

(*
## TCollisionWorld.FromStore
```pascal
function TCollisionWorld.FromStore(var store: TCacheStore): TCollisionWorld; static;
```
The bounds of every region index 5 declares below `REGION_MAX_ID`.

The Java measures its bounds over the regions that actually *loaded*, which is
a smaller set in principle - an archive with no terrain file would be listed
here and absent there. On cache 2625 the two agree exactly: 2934 archives, one
of which is 25287 and excluded by the id bound, leaving the 2933 regions the
committed zip holds. A cache where they disagree wants
{ref}`TCollisionWorld.Create` with explicit numbers.
*)
function TCollisionWorld.FromStore(var store: TCacheStore): TCollisionWorld; static;
var
  table: TCacheRefTable;
  i, id, x, y, minX, minY, maxX, maxY: Integer;
begin
  table := store.GetRefTable(Ord(EIndexType.MAPS));
  if table.Named then
    raise 'Collision: index 5 is named (an OpenRS2-style dump); only the ' +
          'unnamed live-cache layout is implemented';

  minX := -1;
  for i := 0 to High(table.Archives) do
  begin
    id := table.Archives[i].Id;
    if (id < 0) or (id >= REGION_MAX_ID) then Continue;

    x := RegionXOf(id);
    y := RegionYOf(id);
    if minX = -1 then
    begin
      minX := x; maxX := x;
      minY := y; maxY := y;
      Continue;
    end;

    if x < minX then minX := x;
    if x > maxX then maxX := x;
    if y < minY then minY := y;
    if y > maxY then maxY := y;
  end;

  if minX = -1 then
    raise 'Collision: index 5 declares no regions';

  Result := TCollisionWorld.Create(minX, minY, maxX, maxY);
end;

type
(*
## TCollisionObjects
```pascal
TCollisionObjects = record
  SizeX, SizeY, InteractType, WallOrDoor: TIntegerArray;  // indexed by object id
  HasModels, Known: TBooleanArray;
  MaxId: Integer;
end;
```
`ObjectManager` reduced to the five fields the collision pass reads, in one
flat table indexed by object id.

`ObjectManager.load` decodes the whole OBJECT archive up front and
`getObject(id)` is a map lookup (`ObjectManager.java:54-72`); doing the same
here is not just faithful, it is the only affordable shape - the dump asks
about a location's object millions of times, and a by-id lookup through
`TCacheDefs` rescans the reference table on every call.

`HasModels` stands for `getObjectModels() != null`: `TObjectDefinition` uses an
empty array for Java's null, which is exact because the loader only ever
allocates that array from a non-zero length.
*)
  TCollisionObjects = record
    SizeX, SizeY, InteractType, WallOrDoor: TIntegerArray;
    HasModels, Known: TBooleanArray;
    MaxId: Integer;
  end;

(*
## TCollisionObjects.Create
```pascal
function TCollisionObjects.Create(var store: TCacheStore): TCollisionObjects; static;
```
Decodes every OBJECT definition in `store` and keeps the five fields the
renderer needs.

`store` is `var` so the reference table it parses lands on the caller's record
rather than on a temporary copy.
*)
function TCollisionObjects.Create(var store: TCacheStore): TCollisionObjects; static;
var
  files: array of TByteArray;
  table: TCacheRefTable;
  ids: TIntegerArray;
  obj: TObjectDefinition;
  a, i, id: Integer;
begin
  table := store.GetRefTable(Ord(EIndexType.CONFIGS));
  a := table.IndexOfArchive(Ord(EConfigType.OBJECTS));
  if a = -1 then
    raise 'Collision: the cache has no OBJECT config archive';

  ids := table.Archives[a].FileIds;
  if Length(ids) = 0 then
    raise 'Collision: the OBJECT config archive is empty';

  files := store.ReadFiles(Ord(EIndexType.CONFIGS), Ord(EConfigType.OBJECTS), []);

  Result.MaxId := 0;
  for i := 0 to High(ids) do
    if ids[i] > Result.MaxId then Result.MaxId := ids[i];

  SetLength(Result.SizeX, Result.MaxId + 1);
  SetLength(Result.SizeY, Result.MaxId + 1);
  SetLength(Result.InteractType, Result.MaxId + 1);
  SetLength(Result.WallOrDoor, Result.MaxId + 1);
  SetLength(Result.HasModels, Result.MaxId + 1);
  SetLength(Result.Known, Result.MaxId + 1);

  for i := 0 to High(files) do
  begin
    id := ids[i];
    obj := TObjectDefinition.Load(id, files[i]);
    Result.SizeX[id] := obj.SizeX;
    Result.SizeY[id] := obj.SizeY;
    Result.InteractType[id] := obj.InteractType;
    Result.WallOrDoor[id] := obj.WallOrDoor;
    Result.HasModels[id] := Length(obj.ObjectModels) > 0;
    Result.Known[id] := True;
  end;
end;

type
(*
## TCollisionMap
```pascal
TCollisionMap = record
  RegionX, RegionY: Integer;
  DrawBaseX, DrawBaseY: Integer;   // this region's top-left tile on the canvas
  World: TCollisionWorld;
  Settings: TIntegerArray;         // TRegion.TileIndex order
  Locs: array of TLocation;
  TileStart, Order, Occupied: TIntegerArray;
end;
```
One region prepared for rendering: its settings bytes, its locations, and an
index that hands back the locations sitting on one tile.

`TileStart`/`Order` are a CSR-style bucket index over `Locs`, keyed by
`x * 64 + y`, built stably so that the locations of a tile come back in the
order the region file listed them. That is exactly the order
`drawObjects` visits them in - it walks `localX` outermost, `localY` innermost,
and rescans the whole location list per tile (`:363-383`) - at 4096 times less
work. `Occupied` lists the tiles that hold any location at all, ascending, so
the object pass skips the empty ones without losing that order.
*)
  TCollisionMap = record
    RegionX, RegionY: Integer;
    DrawBaseX, DrawBaseY: Integer;
    World: TCollisionWorld;
    Settings: TIntegerArray;
    Locs: array of TLocation;
    TileStart, Order, Occupied: TIntegerArray;
  end;

(*
## TCollisionMap.Create
```pascal
function TCollisionMap.Create(region: TRegion; world: TCollisionWorld): TCollisionMap; static;
```
Prepares `region` for rendering onto the canvas `world` describes.

Raises on a region with no terrain: a caller sweeping an id space should test
`HasTerrain` and skip, rather than render a blank chunk that looks plausible.
*)
function TCollisionMap.Create(region: TRegion; world: TCollisionWorld): TCollisionMap; static;
var
  cursor: TIntegerArray;
  tiles, i, n, t, used: Integer;
begin
  if not region.HasTerrain then
    raise 'Collision: region ' + ToStr(region.Id) + ' has no terrain';

  Result.RegionX := region.RegionX;
  Result.RegionY := region.RegionY;
  Result.World := world;

  // :266-274. drawBaseY inverts Y because the top-most region has the greatest
  // baseY while the top-most image row is 0.
  Result.DrawBaseX := region.BaseX - world.MinRegionX * REGION_X;
  Result.DrawBaseY := world.MaxRegionY * REGION_Y - region.BaseY;

  SetLength(Result.Settings, REGION_Z * REGION_X * REGION_Y);
  for i := 0 to High(Result.Settings) do
    Result.Settings[i] := region.Tiles[i].Settings;

  Result.Locs := Copy(region.Locations);
  n := Length(Result.Locs);
  tiles := REGION_X * REGION_Y;

  SetLength(cursor, tiles);
  for i := 0 to n - 1 do
    Inc(cursor[Result.Locs[i].X * REGION_Y + Result.Locs[i].Y]);

  SetLength(Result.TileStart, tiles + 1);
  t := 0;
  used := 0;
  for i := 0 to tiles - 1 do
  begin
    Result.TileStart[i] := t;
    t += cursor[i];
    if cursor[i] > 0 then Inc(used);
  end;
  Result.TileStart[tiles] := t;

  SetLength(Result.Occupied, used);
  used := 0;
  for i := 0 to tiles - 1 do
    if Result.TileStart[i] <> Result.TileStart[i + 1] then
    begin
      Result.Occupied[used] := i;
      Inc(used);
    end;

  // cursor is reused as a write head per bucket, which is what keeps the
  // within-tile order equal to the region file's order.
  for i := 0 to tiles - 1 do
    cursor[i] := Result.TileStart[i];

  SetLength(Result.Order, n);
  for i := 0 to n - 1 do
  begin
    t := Result.Locs[i].X * REGION_Y + Result.Locs[i].Y;
    Result.Order[cursor[t]] := i;
    Inc(cursor[t]);
  end;
end;

(*
## TCollisionMap.ChunkName
```pascal
function TCollisionMap.ChunkName(z: Integer): String;
```
The zip entry name for plane `z`: `<plane>-<regionX>-<regionY>.png` (`:291`).
This exact spelling is what WaspLib's map loader looks chunks up by - see
`main/position/map/maploader.simba`.
*)
function TCollisionMap.ChunkName(z: Integer): String;
begin
  Result := ToStr(z) + '-' + ToStr(Self.RegionX) + '-' + ToStr(Self.RegionY) + '.png';
end;

(*
## TCollisionMap.InsideMainland
```pascal
function TCollisionMap.InsideMainland(z: Integer): Boolean;
```
`:283-286`. Plane 0 and region x 15..62, y 32..65. A chunk that is *not* inside
it and came out fully walkable is shipped as an all-black image instead - see
{ref}`TCollisionMap.Render`.
*)
function TCollisionMap.InsideMainland(z: Integer): Boolean;
begin
  Result := (z = 0) and
            (Self.RegionX >= COLLISION_MAINLAND_X1) and (Self.RegionX <= COLLISION_MAINLAND_X2) and
            (Self.RegionY >= COLLISION_MAINLAND_Y1) and (Self.RegionY <= COLLISION_MAINLAND_Y2);
end;

(*
## _CollisionPut
```pascal
function _CollisionPut(data: PColorBGRA; originX, originY, gx, gy, r, g, b: Integer): Boolean;
```
One `image.setRGB(gx, gy, ...)` on the whole-map canvas, clipped to the chunk
whose top-left canvas pixel is `(originX, originY)`. Returns whether the pixel
landed inside the chunk, which is what {ref}`TCollisionMap.RenderRaw` uses to
answer "did anything but `walkableColor` get written".

A write that falls outside is dropped rather than raising: the Java's canvas is
the whole map, so those pixels are a *neighbour's* problem, and by the argument
in the unit docstring the neighbour never shows them either.
*)
function _CollisionPut(data: PColorBGRA; originX, originY, gx, gy, r, g, b: Integer): Boolean;
var
  lx, ly: Integer;
begin
  lx := gx - originX;
  if (lx < 0) or (lx >= COLLISION_CHUNK_SIZE) then Exit(False);
  ly := gy - originY;
  if (ly < 0) or (ly >= COLLISION_CHUNK_SIZE) then Exit(False);

  lx := ly * COLLISION_CHUNK_SIZE + lx;
  data[lx]^.R := r;
  data[lx]^.G := g;
  data[lx]^.B := b;
  data[lx]^.A := 255;
  Result := True;
end;

(*
## TCollisionMap.RenderRaw
```pascal
function TCollisionMap.RenderRaw(z: Integer; objs: TCollisionObjects; var walkableOnly: Boolean): TImage;
```
Plane `z` as a 256x256 chunk, **without** the `BLACK_CHUNK` substitution.
`walkableOnly` comes back True when not one pixel other than `walkableColor`
was written, which is `isFullyWhite` (`:243-253`) answered for free instead of
by a second pass over 65536 pixels.

The image starts as a native `Fill` of `walkableColor`, so the terrain pass
only has to write the blocked tiles - about a fifth of them, measured over the
committed zip.
*)
function TCollisionMap.RenderRaw(z: Integer; objs: TCollisionObjects; var walkableOnly: Boolean): TImage;
var
  data: PColorBGRA;
  planeSize, base, originX, originY: Integer;
  x, y, ty, idx, setting, upper, i, j, p, col, row: Integer;
  oi, tileIdx, localX, localY, tileZ, k, li: Integer;
  id, locType, rotation, drawX, drawY, xSize, ySize: Integer;
  r, g, b, gy, lx, ly, lx0, lx1, ly0, ly1: Integer;
begin
  if (z < 0) or (z >= REGION_Z) then
    raise 'Collision: plane ' + ToStr(z) + ' is out of range';

  Result := new TImage(COLLISION_CHUNK_SIZE, COLLISION_CHUNK_SIZE);
  Result.Fill($FFFFFF);
  data := Result.Data;
  walkableOnly := True;

  planeSize := REGION_X * REGION_Y;
  base := z * planeSize;
  originX := Self.DrawBaseX * COLLISION_SCALE;
  originY := Self.DrawBaseY * COLLISION_SCALE;

  // --- terrain, :298-326 ------------------------------------------------
  for x := 0 to REGION_X - 1 do
  begin
    col := x * COLLISION_SCALE;
    for y := 0 to REGION_Y - 1 do
    begin
      // Image row y holds tile (x, 63 - y): image space has a top-left origin
      // and game space a bottom-left one.
      ty := REGION_Y - y - 1;
      idx := x * REGION_Y + ty;
      setting := Self.Settings[base + idx];

      if (setting and 24) = 0 then
        if z = 0 then
        begin
          upper := Self.Settings[planeSize + idx];
          // A bridge deck: plane 0 is drawn with plane 1's walkability.
          if (upper and 2) <> 0 then setting := upper;
        end;

      if (setting and 1) = 0 then Continue;

      walkableOnly := False;
      row := y * COLLISION_SCALE;
      for j := 0 to COLLISION_SCALE - 1 do
      begin
        p := (row + j) * COLLISION_CHUNK_SIZE + col;
        for i := 0 to COLLISION_SCALE - 1 do
        begin
          data[p + i]^.R := 0;
          data[p + i]^.G := 0;
          data[p + i]^.B := 0;
          data[p + i]^.A := 255;
        end;
      end;
    end;
  end;

  // --- objects, :355-539 ------------------------------------------------
  for oi := 0 to High(Self.Occupied) do
  begin
    tileIdx := Self.Occupied[oi];
    localX := tileIdx div REGION_Y;
    localY := tileIdx - localX * REGION_Y;

    // :381. A roofed or hidden tile selects no locations at all.
    if (Self.Settings[base + tileIdx] and 24) <> 0 then Continue;

    // :372-374. Locations on a bridge deck belong to the plane above.
    tileZ := z;
    if (Self.Settings[planeSize + tileIdx] and 2) <> 0 then tileZ += 1;

    for k := Self.TileStart[tileIdx] to Self.TileStart[tileIdx + 1] - 1 do
    begin
      li := Self.Order[k];
      if Self.Locs[li].Plane <> tileZ then Continue;

      locType := Self.Locs[li].LocType;
      // :390-392. Wall decorations do not block.
      if (locType >= 4) and (locType <= 8) then Continue;

      id := Self.Locs[li].Id;
      if (id < 0) or (id > objs.MaxId) or (not objs.Known[id]) then
        raise 'Collision: region ' + ToStr(Self.RegionX) + ',' + ToStr(Self.RegionY) +
              ' places object ' + ToStr(id) + ', which the cache does not hold';

      if objs.InteractType[id] = 0 then Continue;                      // :394-395
      if (locType = 22) and (objs.InteractType[id] <> 1) then Continue; // :421-423

      rotation := Self.Locs[li].Orientation;
      drawX := (Self.DrawBaseX + localX) * COLLISION_SCALE;
      drawY := (Self.DrawBaseY + (REGION_Y - objs.SizeY[id] - localY)) * COLLISION_SCALE;

      if (locType >= 0) and (locType <= 3) then
      begin
        if id = COLLISION_WINTERTODT_ID then Continue;                 // :431-433

        r := 0; g := 0; b := 0;                                        // wallColor
        if objs.WallOrDoor[id] <> 0 then r := 255;                     // doorColor

        // :437. Canvas bounds, not chunk bounds.
        if (drawX < 0) or (drawY < 0) or
           (drawX >= Self.World.Width) or (drawY >= Self.World.Height) then Continue;

        // :439-449. The `Close`-op else-branch at :451-459 is dead - see the
        // unit docstring - so only this arm exists.
        if (locType = 0) or (locType = 2) then
          for i := 0 to COLLISION_SCALE - 1 do
            case rotation of
              0: if _CollisionPut(data, originX, originY, drawX, drawY + i, r, g, b) then walkableOnly := False;
              1: if _CollisionPut(data, originX, originY, drawX + i, drawY, r, g, b) then walkableOnly := False;
              2: if _CollisionPut(data, originX, originY, drawX + COLLISION_SCALE - 1, drawY + i, r, g, b) then walkableOnly := False;
              3: if _CollisionPut(data, originX, originY, drawX + i, drawY + COLLISION_SCALE - 1, r, g, b) then walkableOnly := False;
            end;

        if locType = 3 then                                            // :462-468
          case rotation of
            0: if _CollisionPut(data, originX, originY, drawX, drawY, r, g, b) then walkableOnly := False;
            1: if _CollisionPut(data, originX, originY, drawX + COLLISION_SCALE - 1, drawY, r, g, b) then walkableOnly := False;
            2: if _CollisionPut(data, originX, originY, drawX + COLLISION_SCALE - 1, drawY + COLLISION_SCALE - 1, r, g, b) then walkableOnly := False;
            3: if _CollisionPut(data, originX, originY, drawX, drawY + COLLISION_SCALE - 1, r, g, b) then walkableOnly := False;
          end;

        // :470-478. A type 2 draws a SECOND edge, and its rotations index the
        // four edges differently from the block above.
        if locType = 2 then
          for i := 0 to COLLISION_SCALE - 1 do
            case rotation of
              0: if _CollisionPut(data, originX, originY, drawX + i, drawY, r, g, b) then walkableOnly := False;
              1: if _CollisionPut(data, originX, originY, drawX + COLLISION_SCALE - 1, drawY + i, r, g, b) then walkableOnly := False;
              2: if _CollisionPut(data, originX, originY, drawX + i, drawY + COLLISION_SCALE - 1, r, g, b) then walkableOnly := False;
              3: if _CollisionPut(data, originX, originY, drawX, drawY + i, r, g, b) then walkableOnly := False;
            end;

        Continue;
      end;

      if locType = 9 then                                              // :483-496
      begin
        r := 0; g := 0; b := 0;
        if objs.WallOrDoor[id] <> 0 then r := 255;

        for i := 0 to COLLISION_SCALE - 1 do
        begin
          if (rotation <> 0) and (rotation <> 2) then gy := drawY + i
          else gy := drawY + (COLLISION_SCALE - 1 - i);
          if _CollisionPut(data, originX, originY, drawX + i, gy, r, g, b) then
            walkableOnly := False;
        end;

        Continue;
      end;

      if (locType < 10) or (locType > 22) then Continue;                // :498

      if not objs.HasModels[id] then Continue;                          // :501
      if drawX < 0 then Continue;                                       // :503

      xSize := objs.SizeX[id] * COLLISION_SCALE;
      ySize := objs.SizeY[id] * COLLISION_SCALE;

      // :507-519. Note the two guards `continue` the whole location, so a
      // failure here also skips the transposed block below.
      if (xSize = ySize) or (rotation = 0) or (rotation = 2) then
      begin
        if drawX + xSize >= Self.World.Width then Continue;
        if drawY + ySize >= Self.World.Height then Continue;

        lx0 := drawX - originX;
        lx1 := lx0 + xSize - 1;
        ly0 := drawY - originY;
        ly1 := ly0 + ySize - 1;
        if lx0 < 0 then lx0 := 0;
        if ly0 < 0 then ly0 := 0;
        if lx1 > COLLISION_CHUNK_SIZE - 1 then lx1 := COLLISION_CHUNK_SIZE - 1;
        if ly1 > COLLISION_CHUNK_SIZE - 1 then ly1 := COLLISION_CHUNK_SIZE - 1;

        for ly := ly0 to ly1 do
        begin
          p := ly * COLLISION_CHUNK_SIZE;
          for lx := lx0 to lx1 do
            // `!= wallColor`: black is the only one of the four colours with a
            // zero red channel.
            if data[p + lx]^.R <> 0 then
            begin
              data[p + lx]^.R := 51;
              data[p + lx]^.G := 51;
              data[p + lx]^.B := 51;
              data[p + lx]^.A := 255;
              walkableOnly := False;
            end;
        end;
      end;

      // :521-533. Independent of the block above: a SQUARE object at rotation
      // 1 or 3 runs both.
      if (rotation = 1) or (rotation = 3) then
      begin
        drawY := drawY + ySize - 1;
        if drawY >= Self.World.Height then Continue;
        if drawY - xSize < 0 then Continue;
        if drawX + ySize >= Self.World.Width then Continue;

        // (drawX + sY, drawY - sX) over sX < xSize, sY < ySize covers this
        // rectangle exactly once, so it is a plain fill.
        lx0 := drawX - originX;
        lx1 := lx0 + ySize - 1;
        ly1 := drawY - originY;
        ly0 := ly1 - xSize + 1;
        if lx0 < 0 then lx0 := 0;
        if ly0 < 0 then ly0 := 0;
        if lx1 > COLLISION_CHUNK_SIZE - 1 then lx1 := COLLISION_CHUNK_SIZE - 1;
        if ly1 > COLLISION_CHUNK_SIZE - 1 then ly1 := COLLISION_CHUNK_SIZE - 1;

        for ly := ly0 to ly1 do
        begin
          p := ly * COLLISION_CHUNK_SIZE;
          for lx := lx0 to lx1 do
            if data[p + lx]^.R <> 0 then
            begin
              data[p + lx]^.R := 51;
              data[p + lx]^.G := 51;
              data[p + lx]^.B := 51;
              data[p + lx]^.A := 255;
              walkableOnly := False;
            end;
        end;
      end;
    end;
  end;
end;

(*
## TCollisionMap.Render
```pascal
function TCollisionMap.Render(z: Integer; objs: TCollisionObjects): TImage;
```
Plane `z` exactly as `collision.zip` carries it: {ref}`TCollisionMap.RenderRaw`
plus the `BLACK_CHUNK` substitution (`:283-290`) - a fully walkable chunk
outside the mainland box is shipped as an all-black image.
*)
function TCollisionMap.Render(z: Integer; objs: TCollisionObjects): TImage;
var
  walkableOnly: Boolean;
begin
  Result := Self.RenderRaw(z, objs, walkableOnly);
  if walkableOnly and (not Self.InsideMainland(z)) then
    Result.Fill($000000);
end;
